  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern this morning is dominated by a\nridge of high pressure across the Desert Southwest with an \nupper-high center positioned over west-central Arizona. The \nlatest GOES precipitable water imagery still continues to depict\ndeep moisture across all of Southeast Arizona with PWATs \n1.50-1.75 west of Tucson and 1.25-1.50 elsewhere. \n\nAlthough the 850-700 mb theta-e axis extends across our CWA, \nwhat we have in store for today is less instability and the \nlack of decent deformation/lift aloft. The 500 mb high center \nwill make its way from west-central Arizona into southern \nArizona reducing mid-level lapse rates and leaving only extreme\nSE AZ and the southern AZ/NM border in a weakly favorable \ndeformation field aloft. I would expect to see less coverage of\nstorms than Wednesday with generally isolated to scattered \nthunderstorms, especially across Cochise county to the \nsoutheast of Tucson. Given the deeper moisture, heavy rainfall \nwill remain as the primary threat from thunderstorms today. \n\nThunderstorm activity will increase again Friday, especially\nacross south-central Arizona. The combination of deformation/lift\naloft developing in the wake (west side) of the 500 mb high \ncenter moving into SW NM along and decent moisture in place \nwith PWATs 1.5-2.0 standard deviations above the normal, we \nshould have the potential for some pretty decent rainfall/ \nlocalized flash flooding to the south of Tucson. In fact, the \n10/00Z HREF indicates a neighborhood probability of 50-70 \npercent above 1 inch of rainfall on their 3-hr PMM for 12/00Z \nFriday afternoon and even 10 percent above 3 inches. I \ncoordinated with the WPC to add a slight risk of rainfall \nexceeding flash flood guidance to their Day 2 (Friday) Excessive\nRainfall Outlook (ERO) to south-central Arizona (south of \nTucson).\n\nOtherwise, decent moisture will remain in place across \nSoutheast Arizona this weekend into the middle of next week. The\nsubtropical ridge begins to shift east into the southeastern \nstates leaving our neck of the woods along the western \nperiphery. This will result in generally isolated to scattered \nthunderstorms, mainly from central Pima County eastward with \nheavy rainfall as the primary hazard. An inverted trough may \nfind its way around the subtropical ridge into SE AZ Monday, for\na better coverage of storms and a higher probability for heavy \nrainfall/isolated flash flooding.",
